# Selenium XPath Debugging

When the user asks to inspect, debug, verify, or fix an XPath or CSS selector — or reports an element not being found — **always use the `user-selenium` MCP tools** to interact with the live browser. Do not guess or rely solely on static code analysis.

## Default Workflow

1. `start_browser` if no session is active (check with `diagnostics` first).
2. `navigate` to the relevant URL (course page, lab environment tab, etc.).
3. `execute_script` to run JS queries (`document.querySelector`, `document.evaluate`, etc.) and inspect real DOM structure.
4. `take_screenshot` to visually confirm what the page looks like before and after.
5. Use findings to update the XPath/selector in the Python source.

## Key MCP Tools

| Tool | When to use |
|---|---|
| `execute_script` | Query DOM, inspect attributes, simulate clicks via JS |
| `take_screenshot` | Visual verification of page state |
| `interact` | Click elements, fill inputs |
| `navigate` | Go to a specific URL |
| `diagnostics` | Check if a browser session already exists |

## Notes

- Factory environment (`rol-factory.ole.redhat.com`) requires GitHub 2FA — the user must complete login manually if not already authenticated.
- ROL environment (`rol.redhat.com`) authenticates via Red Hat SSO / VPN automatically.
- After login, always scroll to the relevant section before inspecting elements.
